{"episodes":{"183034":"A cutoff low produced multiple days of heavy rain. Hazards that resulted included gusty severe winds, flooding, funnel clouds, hail, and tree damage.","183109":"A severe thunderstorm tracked across Scotts Bluff County during the afternoon of 9/4.","183246":"Thunderstorms tracking across eastern Oglala Lakota County produced a few wind gusts to around 60 mph.","183590":"A severe thunderstorm developed over northeast Crook County, producing large hail east of Aladdin.","183591":"A line of severe thunderstorms developed over far northeast Wyoming and tracked northeast across northwest South Dakota, producing hail and wind gusts to around 70 mph.","183599":"A thunderstorm became severe over the Rapid City area, then moved northeast through southern Meade County, producing large hail and wind gusts to over 70 mph. A likely microburst in the northeast Rapid City and Box Elder areas downed numerous power poles and damaged a mobile home.","183851":"An active pattern resulted in strong wind gusts in addition to isolated flash flooding to start the month of September.","184403":"A potent disturbance impacted the Northern Rockies Sunday, bringing showers and thunderstorms to Southwest and portions of North Central Montana throughout the afternoon hours.","184847":"With abnormal heat and humidity across the area for early September, isolated thunderstorms developed during the early morning hours. One of these storms generated a heat burst near the town of Redfield. The Automated Weather Observing System (AWOS) at the Redfield airport recorded a wind gust of 57 mph, with the associated winds causing some roofing material to get blown off a trailer 4 miles south of Redfield. This heat burst also resulted in a temperature jump from 73 degrees to 84 degrees in just 20 minutes at the AWOS.","184849":"A weak low pressure system and cold front helped to generate strong storms in north central and south central South Dakota during the late afternoon and evening hours of September 4th. The strongest recorded wind gust with any of these storms was 70 mph on a personal weather station at a residence 7 miles south of Morristown, SD.","185284":"A storm complex moved from northwest to southeast across the region producing widespread wind damage on portions of central into south central Missouri.","185621":"An area of thunderstorms stretching from far southeast Montana towards southwest South Dakota developed ahead of an upper level trough, which moved to the northeast towards North Dakota. As the storms pushed into North Dakota, they encountered an atmosphere of elevated instability and strong deep layer shear. Strong and damaging wind gusts quickly became the key threat with these storms as they progressed to the northeast, though a few storms did produce large hail, with the largest hail being half-dollar size in McLean County. Multiple events of wind gusts over 70 mph occurred. The strongest wind gust estimated at 100 mph occurred in far northwest Kidder County, where neighboring farmsteads sustained heavy damage with a destroyed mobile home and grain bin, a severely damaged shop building, and multiple large trees either uprooted or snapped.","185694":"Scattered thunderstorms occurred across southeast Montana, with a couple producing quarter size hail and a severe wind gust.","185901":"An upper level low pressure center situated over North Texas resulted in isolated to scattered thunderstorm development across North and East east Texas the afternoon of Sunday September 3. A few storms produced damaging winds and hail.","186059":"Water ran across Old Highway 191 at several locations between Idaho 36 and Devil Creek Reservoir due to heavy rain.","186088":"Isolated thunderstorm over North Washington caused minor damage and power outages.","186107":"An isolated gale-force wind gust associated with a thunderstorm was observed south of Marathon.  The thunderstorm was embedded in stronger easterly lower tropospheric flow south of a strong high pressure ridge.","186143":"An upper level low over northern California yielded a cooler,  wetter, and breezy pattern across the region through Labor Day weekend. Periods of light to moderate precipitation intermingled with isolated thunderstorms produced brief periods of heavy rainfall across the Sierra into northwest Nevada that caused some flash flooding and flooding concerns through the Labor weekend. Of particular concern was flooding in the playa area of the Black Rock Desert during the Burning Man festival. A few isolated severe thunderstorms occurred across parts of the Basin and Range east of US-95 in Pershing, Churchill, and Mineral counties as well.","186161":"Fueled by an abundance of moisture, a thunderstorm complex developed during peak heating over the Pearl River basin. Aided by a very weak steering flow, the complex moved very slowly over portions of urbanized areas such as Slidell and eventually metro New Orleans, where flash flooding occurred during the afternoon and evening. The complex finally weakened during the evening as it became cold-pool dominant.","187141":"A marginally severe environment resulted in generally disorganized scattered convection in the late afternoon. A weak convective line and separate cell produced severe wind gusts and isolated damage in portions of southeast and east central Missouri, respectively."},"events":[{"begin":"2023-09-04T00:09Z","county":"Fergus","deaths":0,"end":"2023-09-04T00:09Z","endLat":47.03,"endLon":-109.47,"episode":184403,"id":1128839,"injuries":0,"lat":47.03,"location":"1.38 S (Lwt)Lewiston Muni A","lon":-109.47,"magnitude":52.0,"magnitudeType":"MG","narrative":"ASOS station KLWT Lewistown Airport. Peak wind gust associated with outflow boundary.","source":"ASOS","state":"MT","type":"Thunderstorm Wind","wfo":"TFX"},{"begin":"2023-09-04T00:11Z","county":"Mineral","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2023-09-04T07:11Z","endLat":38.4242,"endLon":-118.6011,"episode":